[list]
[*]Three new styles of floor border: an inlay, one fine line set in from the edge; a carved vine; and pebbles set in grout, built from stone, for paths outdoors. The vine and the pebbles come in four patterns each, and every tile of border draws one by where it lies, so a long run never repeats on a beat. Paint colours only the vine itself, leaves and stem, so a wooden vine painted green is a green vine on a wooden border.
[*]A diagonal piece in every style, for diagonal walls such as Diagonal Walls 2's. Build it on the wall's own tile, under the wall, and rotate it to put the band on the open side. It joins the borders beside it by itself: square along a run of diagonals, and mitred where the line turns into a straight or another diagonal. It keeps its whole tile crisp, which stops the ground under the outer row of a staggered diagonal wall creeping into the room.
[*]Each shape's build button on the Floors tab now holds every style. Left-click builds the style shown on the button; right-click lists the others.
[*]Floor borders are drawn from one strip texture per style, laid out as geometry, instead of a texture for every shape and facing. The original pieces look the same, except where two of a piece's bands meet at a corner: the joint is now cut at 45 degrees, which changes a few pixels at the corner's tip, groove and inner lip.
[*]They cost less to draw. Every floor border of one style in a map section draws in a single call, in any mix of materials and paint, where 1.0.1 took 21 calls for a section holding every shape at every rotation, or 24 with mixed materials and paint. The four styles' 32 textures take 1.53 MB of video memory; 1.0.1's one style took 24 textures and 2.1 MB.
[*]One Harmony patch fewer. Neat Edges no longer patches the game's texture atlas, which ran each time the game offered a texture to the atlas at startup and existed only to keep the borders' textures out of it.
[*]Other mods can draw their own trims the same way, diagonals, patterned strips and paint on part of a trim included, through the graphic class and shape extension the floor borders use. The README on GitHub has the details.
[*]Unchanged: the six original pieces cost the same and harden the same edges, and borders already built in your saves load as they are.
[/list]
